<%# == The unannotated tests THEMSELVES, for the place the reader picked
        The last rung of the ladder the Overview panel opens and "How SpecGuard reads this suite, by
        area" narrows. The Overview used to print "Not visible to SpecGuard" as `total_specs_count -
        annotated_specs_count` and say *"SpecGuard cannot see the other N tests"*; the ranking above
        names the AREAS that count is concentrated in. Neither names a TEST, and until this panel the
        only route to one was `GET /api/v1/repository?unannotated_examples=1&spec_directory=…` with
        an API key — a route off the product, for the reader the product had just handed a worklist
        to.

        An area picked from the ranking then had its own gap, the same one one rung up had: the
        worklist below is a capped hundred ordered file-navigably, so a file whose rows fall past
        the hundredth was named nowhere on the page. The "Files in this area holding unannotated
        tests" table at the top of this panel is that rung — every row a `?spec_file=` away from a
        worklist that starts at that file's own rows — and it renders only for a directory ask,
        the only ask it means anything under.

        SPGD-711 then corrected what may be said ABOUT these rows without changing which rows they
        are. Every example here lacks an authored `@intent` — exact, and the same predicate the
        Overview subtracts. Most of them SpecGuard nonetheless READS, from the description the client
        sends and this platform stores; the last column shows exactly what it read, so a reader can
        check the reading rather than take a badge's word for it. The rows it read nothing from come
        FIRST, because the list is capped at a hundred and those are the ones that must not fall off
        the page.

        Rendered only when a narrowing was ASKED for, and the read is taken only then. The model's
        narrowings are OPTIONAL and whole-run is a complete ask on the JSON endpoint; this surface
        deliberately does not take it, for the reason the controller states: a hundred rows of debt
        nobody asked for is the Overview's subtraction again at length.

        NO NEW PARAMETER. It rides `?spec_directory=` and `?spec_file=`, so one ask opens EVERY
        panel that reads it, each answering in its own grain over the same area. `show.html.erb`
        states the rule at "Areas that grew or shrank": that is how `drill_down_path` composes asks,
        and it is not a collision to be fixed by minting a second parameter.

        NO "Close" BUTTON, and its absence is a decision rather than an omission. The three panels
        that carry one each OWN an ask, and closing it is the gesture that dismisses them. This panel
        owns neither of the asks it rides: a "Close" here would have to clear `?spec_directory=`,
        which would close the two panels that ask belongs to as a side effect — the precise inversion
        of the carry-through rule `RepositoriesHelper#drill_down_path` exists to keep ("closing an
        area is not a request to close the file", and in every other direction). The reader dismisses
        this panel with the Close button of the panel that owns the ask, which is where they set it.

        It PRESENTS and does not judge — the rule `UnannotatedExamples` and `UnannotatedDirectories`
        both state, and it governs this partial too: rows, and never a fraction, a threshold or a
        colour-coded verdict on one of them.

        Same visibility rule and same gate as the panels around it: read-only suite telemetry on
        `current_repository(:view)`, outside `manage_keys`, and nothing here widens that. %>
<% if @unannotated_examples %>
      <% narrowings = [@unannotated_examples.spec_file, @unannotated_examples.spec_directory].compact %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Unannotated tests here", id: "unannotated-examples") do %>
        <% if narrowings.size > 1 %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3">
            Both asks narrow this list and a row must match each of them, so a file outside that area
            is an empty intersection rather than one of the two having been dropped.
          </p>
        <% end %>
        <% if @unannotated_examples.rows.any? %>
          <% if @unannotated_files %>
            <%# == The files of this area that hold the work
                    The rung between the by-area ranking above and the worklist below, and the one
                    the worklist's own cap kept out of reach: a capped hundred ordered
                    file-navigably names the files whose rows fit on the page and nowhere else, so
                    an area carrying more debt than the cap was a reader who already knew the path
                    away from finding the rest of its work. Each row here is a `?spec_file=` away
                    from a worklist that starts at that file's own rows.

                    Rendered only when the worklist below has rows — which is also why there is no
                    empty state here: a row on the worklist is an unannotated example in the area,
                    so at least one of its files carries debt and this table cannot have come back
                    empty. Zero-debt files are omitted by the read's HAVING, on the argument its
                    comment holds — this is a worklist of work, not an inventory of the area.

                    Operands only, no verdict, like every list on this page. %>
            <% worklist_truncated = @unannotated_examples.recorded_count > @unannotated_examples.rows.size %>
            <%= render UI::NoteComponent.new(summary: "How to read this", id: "unannotated-files-basis") do %>
              <span class="font-mono text-app-content-secondary"><%= @unannotated_files.spec_directory %></span>
              —
              <% if @unannotated_files.truncated? %>
                The <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_files.rows.size) %> files holding the
                most unannotated tests, of the
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_files.file_count) %> in this area that hold
                any — most unreadable tests first, then most unannotated, the same ranking the
                by-area table above uses.
              <% else %>
                All <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_files.file_count) %>
                <%= "spec file".pluralize(@unannotated_files.file_count) %> in this area that hold
                at least one unannotated test — most unreadable tests first, then most unannotated,
                the same ranking the by-area table above uses.
              <% end %>
              A file with none is not listed: this is where this area's work sits, not an inventory
              of it — “Spec files in this directory”, open on the same ask, names what the run
              recorded there.
              Operands, never a percentage: a file at 40 of 40 unannotated is equally a file nobody
              has annotated yet and a file of generated specs nobody intends to, and nothing on
              this page decides which.
              <% if worklist_truncated %>
                <%# Which of the files LISTED HERE the worklist's hundred rows actually carry —
                    the overlap, not the count of files on the page: "reaches 100" next to a
                    ten-row table would be arithmetic without a subject. A listed file outside
                    the overlap is the whole reason this rung exists, so the sentence ends by
                    saying where its rows are. %>
                <% worklist_paths = @unannotated_examples.rows.map(&:spec_file_path) %>
                <% reached = @unannotated_files.rows.count { |file| worklist_paths.include?(file.path) } %>
                The worklist below is capped at
                <%= number_with_delimiter(SpecObservation::UNANNOTATED_EXAMPLES_LIMIT) %> rows and
                carries the tests of <%= number_with_delimiter(reached) %> of these
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_files.rows.size) %>
                <%= "file".pluralize(@unannotated_files.rows.size) %> — a file whose tests fall
                past that cap is still listed here, and its link opens a worklist that starts at
                its own rows.
              <% end %>
              With a <code>?layer=</code> ask on the URL these counts ignore it, exactly as the
              worklist below and the by-area ranking above do.
              Counted off the per-example rows this run reported, which is a different population
              from the suite size on the Summary at the top of this page and may legitimately
              differ from it.
            <% end %>
            <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(
                  columns: ["Spec file", { label: "Unannotated", end: true }, { label: "Unreadable", end: true },
                            { label: "Recorded", end: true }],
                  describedby: "unannotated-files-basis", sortable: true, table_id: "unannotated-files") do %>
              <% @unannotated_files.rows.each do |file| %>
                <tr>
                  <td class="font-mono text-xs [overflow-wrap:anywhere]">
                    <%# The link carries BOTH asks explicitly, the resolved value from the row's
                        own panel object rather than the carried request ivar — the rule the
                        area-files table states for the same gesture. %>
                    <%= link_to file.path,
                                drill_down_path(@repository, spec_file: file.path,
                                                spec_directory: @unannotated_files.spec_directory,
                                                anchor: "unannotated-examples"),
                                class: "rc-link" %>
                  </td>
                  <td class="num mute" data-sort="<%= file.unannotated_count %>"><%= number_with_delimiter(file.unannotated_count) %></td>
                  <td class="num" data-sort="<%= file.unreadable_count %>"><%= number_with_delimiter(file.unreadable_count) %></td>
                  <td class="num mute" data-sort="<%= file.recorded_count %>"><%= number_with_delimiter(file.recorded_count) %></td>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
          <% truncated = @unannotated_examples.recorded_count > @unannotated_examples.rows.size %>
          <% derived_on_page = @unannotated_examples.rows.count { |observation| observation.derived_intent } %>
          <% derived_past_cap = @unannotated_examples.derived_count - derived_on_page %>
          <% one_derived = @unannotated_examples.derived_count == 1 %>
          <% one_unreadable = @unannotated_examples.unreadable_count == 1 %>
          <%= render UI::NoteComponent.new(summary: "How to read this", id: "unannotated-examples-basis") do %>
            <% if @unannotated_examples.spec_file %>
              <span class="font-mono text-app-content-secondary"><%= @unannotated_examples.spec_file %></span>
            <% end %>
            <% if narrowings.size > 1 %>and<% end %>
            <% if @unannotated_examples.spec_directory %>
              <span class="font-mono text-app-content-secondary"><%= @unannotated_examples.spec_directory %></span>
            <% end %>
            —
            <% if truncated %>
              The first <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.rows.size) %> of the
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count) %> examples this run
              recorded here without an <code>@intent</code>, in the order you would open them — by
              spec file, then by line.
              The other
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count - @unannotated_examples.rows.size) %>
              are not on this page: this is a worklist capped at
              <%= number_with_delimiter(SpecObservation::UNANNOTATED_EXAMPLES_LIMIT) %>, so it is the
              head of what is unannotated here and not the whole of it.
            <% else %>
              All <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count) %>
              <%= "example".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count) %> this run recorded here
              without an <code>@intent</code>, in the order you would open them — by spec file, then
              by line.
            <% end %>
            Each row names the spec file that RAN the example, and under the name where it is
            DEFINED — the same file for every ordinary example, and a different one for an example a
            shared example group defines elsewhere.
            Named and not scored: nothing here says whether a test ought to carry an
            <code>@intent</code>, only that this run recorded it without one.
            <% if @unannotated_examples.derived_count.zero? %>
              SpecGuard reads none of them from the test's own description either — the last column
              says so row by row. These are the tests it genuinely cannot see: not merely tests
              without an <code>@intent</code>, but tests whose description does not give it an
              entity, an action and a behavior.
            <% elsif @unannotated_examples.unreadable_count.positive? %>
              <% if truncated %>
                SpecGuard reads <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
                of them from the test's own description — a weaker reading than an
                <code>@intent</code>: no preconditions, and a layer inferred from the directory
                rather than declared.
                The other <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.unreadable_count) %>
                it cannot read at all, and <%= one_unreadable ? "that one is" : "those are" %>
                listed first — so the last column shows
                <%= number_with_delimiter(derived_on_page) %> of
                <%= one_derived ? "that" : "those" %>
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
                <%= "reading".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %> on this
                page, and the other
                <%= number_with_delimiter(derived_past_cap) %>
                <%= derived_past_cap == 1 ? "is" : "are" %> past the cap.
              <% else %>
                SpecGuard reads <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
                of them from the test's own description — shown in the last column, and a weaker
                reading than an <code>@intent</code>: no preconditions, and a layer inferred from the
                directory rather than declared.
                The other <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.unreadable_count) %>
                it cannot read at all, and <%= one_unreadable ? "that one is" : "those are" %>
                listed first.
              <% end %>
            <% else %>
              <% if truncated %>
                SpecGuard reads all <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
                <%= "example".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %> here from the test's
                own description — a weaker reading than an <code>@intent</code>: no preconditions,
                and a layer inferred from the directory rather than declared.
                There is none here it cannot read at all.
                The last column shows <%= number_with_delimiter(derived_on_page) %> of
                <%= one_derived ? "that" : "those" %>
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
                <%= "reading".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %> on this
                page, and the other
                <%= number_with_delimiter(derived_past_cap) %>
                <%= derived_past_cap == 1 ? "is" : "are" %> past the cap.
              <% else %>
                SpecGuard reads all <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
                <%= "example".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %> here from the test's
                own description — shown in the last column, and a weaker reading than an
                <code>@intent</code>: no preconditions, and a layer inferred from the directory rather
                than declared.
                There is none here it cannot read at all.
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
            Counted off the per-example rows this run reported, which is a different population from
            the suite size on the Summary at the top of this page and may legitimately differ from it.
          <% end %>
          <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(
                columns: ["Test", "Spec file", "What SpecGuard reads"],
                describedby: "unannotated-examples-basis", sortable: true) do %>
            <% @unannotated_examples.rows.each do |observation| %>
              <% derived = observation.derived_intent %>
              <%= drawer_row(observation.id, kind: "Unannotated test", title: observation.label,
                    facts: [["Defined at", observation.location_label], ["Spec file that ran it", observation.spec_file_path],
                            ["SpecGuard reads", derived ? "#{derived.entity} #{derived.action} — #{derived.behavior}" : "Nothing — no @intent, and the description does not give an entity, an action and a behavior."],
                            ["Layer (inferred from the directory)", derived&.layer]],
                    actions: [["Open on GitHub", @repository.github_blob_url(observation.file_path, observation.line_number, @latest_test_run.commit_sha), :primary]]) do %>
                <td>
                  <%= row_open(observation.label) %>
                  <span class="rc-sec mono"><%= observation.location_label %></span>
                </td>
                <td class="rc-ext" style="font-family: var(--font-mono)"><span class="rc-sec mono" style="margin:0"><%= observation.spec_file_path %></span></td>
                <td class="text-xs">
                  <% if derived %>
                    <span class="rc-intent" style="margin:0"><span class="mono"><%= derived.entity %> <%= derived.action %></span> <%= derived.behavior %></span>
                  <% else %>
                    <span class="text-app-content-secondary">Nothing — no <code>@intent</code>, and the description does not give an entity, an action and a behavior.</span>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% elsif @unannotated_directories&.recorded? %>
          <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                title: "Nothing unannotated here",
                description: "This run recorded no example without an @intent in " \
                             "#{narrowings.to_sentence}. Every example it did record there carries " \
                             "one — and so does a place it recorded nothing in at all, since a " \
                             "population of no examples has no unannotated ones either, and this " \
                             "list cannot tell those two apart. The " \
                             "#{@unannotated_examples.spec_directory ? "“Spec files in this directory”" : "“Examples in this spec file”"} " \
                             "panel, open on the same ask, names what this run actually recorded there."
              ) %>
        <% else %>
          <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                title: "No per-example detail on this run",
                description: "This run reported suite totals without the per-example rows SpecGuard " \
                             "names tests from, so there is nothing to list for " \
                             "#{narrowings.to_sentence} — not that everything there is annotated. " \
                             "Any count on the Summary at the top of this page is taken from the run's own totals."
              ) %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
